---
import "../styles/components/intake-form.css";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Logo from "../components/Logo.astro";
import ClaimsTable from "../components/ClaimsTable.astro";
import IntellectualLineage from "../components/IntellectualLineage.astro";
import AdversariesTable from "../components/positioning/AdversariesTable.astro";
import CompletionsTable from "../components/positioning/CompletionsTable.astro";
import InstrumentsTable from "../components/positioning/InstrumentsTable.astro";
import { cases, type LearningVerdict } from "../content/casebook";
import { studio, studioSummary } from "../content/studio";

// The contribution section states how the casebook's institutions responded,
// so the tally comes from the cases and moves when a case is added.
const learning: Record<LearningVerdict, number> = {
  learned: 0,
  partial: 0,
  absorbed: 0,
};
for (const entry of cases) {
  learning[entry.learningOutcome.verdict] += 1;
}
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Ethotechnics — Institute and Studio"
  description="What the Ethotechnics framework is, where it comes from, and how the open Institute and Studio practice fit together."
  publishedTime="2026-05-26T00:00:00.000Z"
>
  <section class="about-hero stack-lg">
    <div class="about-hero__layout">
      <div class="about-hero__main stack-md">
        <p class="eyebrow">About the framework</p>
        <h1>
          An engineering discipline for keeping automated decisions answerable
        </h1>
        <p class="section-lead">
          Institutions absorb the evidence that their automated systems are
          wrong. A worker quietly fixes the error. An appeal settles one case.
          The rule never changes. Ethotechnics names that mechanism, scores it
          in public cases, and specifies the records that carry the evidence
          back to the rule in any institution that adopts them.
        </p>
        <p class="section-lead">
          Where a dashboard reports accuracy or efficiency as a property of the
          system, the casebook and the standards show it as a distribution among
          people: who carries the errors, who holds the authority, and who can
          make the institution respond.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          The formal definition: keeping capability, authority, evidence,
          dependency, standing, and correction coupled tightly enough that
          increasing machine agency does not silently become unreviewable
          institutional power. Those six are the safeguards the casebook scores.
          The framework is written by <strong
            ><a href="https://kanav.net">Kanav Jain</a></strong
          > as part of the <a href="https://kanav.net/research/"
            >institutional error-accounting research program</a
          >, drawing upon the qualitative diagnostics developed across <a
            href="https://thecrumple.zone">The Crumple Zone</a
          >, and published openly as standards, a glossary, and diagnostics.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="about-hero__flourish" aria-hidden="true">
        <Logo class="about-hero__logo" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="scholars" class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">Nine pairings</p>
    <h2>Shared values, new instruments</h2>
    <p>
      The neighboring fields already hold the commitments: contestability,
      meaningful control, oversight, resilience. What they lack is failure
      mechanics — a way to state when each value decays, what to measure, and
      who would have to act.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each pairing below names one scholar's concept and the instrument on this
      site that measures it. The traditions stay theirs; the instruments are
      what the values were missing.
    </p>
    <InstrumentsTable />
    <p class="small muted">
      Six of the instruments have worked versions — the hospital power audit,
      the corrigibility ladder, the oversight decay test, the role-decomposition
      matrix, the reserve accounting balance sheet, and the
      compensated-performance equation — on the{" "}
      <a href="/research/scholarly-crossings">scholarly crossings</a> page.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="divergence" class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">Eight completions</p>
    <h2>Different mechanisms, one question</h2>
    <p>
      Some of the strongest arguments against new governance prefer an existing
      mechanism: markets, user exit, open models, benefit-cost analysis,
      industry self-regulation, application-layer rules, institutional
      adaptation, or management-based regulation.
    </p>
    <p>
      This framework does not replace any of them. It adds one question each
      mechanism's own theory does not ask: does this mechanism continue to hold
      effective corrective power after the system has been successful for ten
      years? The condition matters. Prolonged success changes the mechanism —
      dependency accumulates, exit narrows, the handler's practice decays — so a
      mechanism that corrects in year one can be nominal by year ten.
    </p>
    <CompletionsTable />
    <p class="small muted">
      None of these additions asks anyone to give up the mechanism. Each one
      names the record that shows whether the mechanism still works.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="disagreement" class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">Five counterpositions</p>
    <h2>The real disagreement</h2>
    <p>
      The serious opponent of this framework does not defend unaccountable
      power. The serious opponent holds that competition, exit, reputation, tort
      liability, sectoral regulation, and ordinary democratic institutions
      already provide sufficient correction.
    </p>
    <p>
      If that is right, this framework is overhead. Its case rests on there
      being identifiable, measurable circumstances in which those mechanisms
      stop providing effective correction as technological dependency grows.
      Each position below is stated so its holders would accept it, and each row
      names the empirical question that would settle it.
    </p>
    <AdversariesTable />
    <p class="small muted">
      The disagreements are empirical, not aesthetic. The{" "}
      <a href="/research/scholarly-crossings#disagreement"
        >scholarly crossings</a
      > note develops each position.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="claims" class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">What it tests, and what it leaves open</p>
    <h2>From a complaint to a record an institution can breach</h2>
    <p>
      Concerns about an automated system often start with the people it decides
      about and the staff who clean up after it. They arrive as complaints, and
      an institution is free to treat a complaint as an anecdote. The standards
      restate each one as a record with an owner and a clock. A nurse's "we're
      constantly rescuing this system" becomes a measured count of the
      correction staff absorb. A claimant's "nobody listens when it gets me
      wrong" becomes standing: a challenge that must be received, answered by a
      date, and able to change the decision.
    </p>
    <p>The same records test what an institution says about itself:</p>
    <ClaimsTable />
    <p>
      The restatement has a limit. It can turn "I am bearing this error" into
      standing against a decision. It cannot settle "why should this institution
      hold this power over me at all." An election can authorize a program; it
      does not give the person the program decides about a way to contest that
      decision. The standards force a named decision. They do not make it, and
      the choices built into them, such as who receives standing and how short a
      clock is, carry values of their own.
    </p>
    <p>
      The method leaves the question of who should hold power out on purpose, so
      that people who disagree about the answer can demand the same records. The
      theory takes it up in
      <a href="/research/theory/deliberate-non-use">deliberate non-use</a>,
      <a href="/research/theory/democratic-vs-coercive-governability"
        >democratic vs. coercive governability</a
      >, and
      <a href="/research/theory/the-compulsion-problem"
        >the compulsion problem</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">What it is for</p>
    <h2>Relying on a system without being ruled by it</h2>
    <p>
      Institutions already rely on things they cannot fully inspect: colleagues,
      suppliers, instruments, the last shift's notes. That reliance is what
      makes an institution more than one person, and nothing here argues against
      it. What the framework is for is keeping reliance on a system from turning
      into subordination to it, in either direction. The institution should be
      able to depend on a system without losing the capacity to question,
      narrow, or replace it. The people the system's decisions land on should be
      able to depend on the institution without losing the standing to object
      and be answered.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every law on the site is a constraint that keeps one of those two
      dependences from converting into command. Capability does not become
      authority; a quiet year does not become renewal; a captive population does
      not become a population that may be directed. The
      <a href="/standards/laws">laws</a> state the constraints, the
      <a href="/standards/core-axioms">axioms</a> state what they are owed to and
      why, and the <a href="/research/theory">theory essays</a> develop the argument.
      A reader can adopt the constraints without the ideal. The ideal is what they
      add up to.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      The compact ambition: systems that can keep becoming more capable without
      becoming more entitled to rule.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">The underlying project</p>
    <h2>
      Power that stays correctable, because every institution gets things wrong
    </h2>
    <p>
      Strip the technology away and the underlying claim concerns the
      relationship among knowledge, power, dependence, burden, and correction.
      The question it answers: what kinds of power does a technical system
      create, who bears its failures, and what prevents that power from becoming
      final? The chain behind it is short. Institutions must represent reality
      to act. Representation is necessarily incomplete. Incompleteness creates
      residual burdens. Those burdens are distributed politically. Power becomes
      dominating when it externalizes them while insulating itself from
      correction.
    </p>
    <p>
      Domination, in this frame, is power insulated from{" "}
      <a href="/glossary/consequential-contradiction"
        >consequential contradiction</a
      >: the institution defines the relevant reality, acts on its definition,
      pushes the residual downward, controls what counts as evidence against
      itself, and cannot be forced to revise by those it gets wrong. The
      epistemology is fallibilist rather than skeptical: knowledge stays
      defeasible because <a href="/glossary/excluded-case">the excluded case</a> is
      potentially evidence about the category, and that fallibilism is material —
      a system is not meaningfully open to correction if the people who know it is
      wrong must destroy themselves to prove it.
    </p>
    <p>
      No arrangement is innocent by design: markets, states, democratic
      majorities, expertise, participation, and emancipatory goals all face the
      same permanent question — whatever arrangement you favor, what happens
      when it gets someone wrong? The five concerns of the design discipline
      follow from that, and each is a working part of this site: jurisdiction (<a
        href="/glossary/non-conversion-principle"
        >what the institution may decide at all</a
      >), representation (<a href="/glossary/safe-incompleteness"
        >what it models and how that can be contested</a
      >), residual custody (<a href="/glossary/error-bearing-party"
        >who carries what it cannot represent</a
      >), corrigibility (<a href="/glossary/exception-learning"
        >whether error travels back into the rule</a
      >), and non-finality (<a href="/glossary/non-finality"
        >whether its decisions stay reopenable</a
      >).
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      The compact form of the whole project: how can institutions be powerful
      enough to act without becoming powerful enough to define away the evidence
      that they are wrong?
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="lineage" class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">Intellectual lineage and distinctive contribution</p>
    <h2>Where the argument comes from, and where it departs</h2>
    <IntellectualLineage />
    <p>
      Its departure is the instrument. The claim becomes obligations a running
      system can fail, records an auditor can read, and public cases scored
      against both:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Obligations with clause numbers:</strong> a grant that expires unless
        renewed on evidence stated in advance (STD-08 §1.1); a widening of scope that
        needs its own authorization (STD-08 §1.3); an objection that owes a revision
        or a reasoned refusal on a clock (STD-07 §4.2); a published measure of the
        time a system costs people (STD-01 §7.1); and standing for whoever absorbs
        its errors (STD-02 §8.4).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Records:</strong> the
        <a href="/standards/std-07-revisable-delegation-record"
          >revisable delegation record</a
        > and the
        <a href="/standards/authority-grant.schema.json"
          >authority grant schema</a
        >, so a reader can check whether each obligation was met.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Public cases:</strong> the <a href="/casebook">casebook</a> scores
        {cases.length} failures on the six safeguards and on what the institution
        did afterward: {learning.absorbed} left the process unchanged, {
          learning.partial
        } partly changed it, and {learning.learned} changed the rules.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small muted">
      In short: the underlying political theory establishes why human endurance
      does not legitimate an institutional arrangement; the engineering method
      proposes ways to measure and prevent the mechanisms through which
      institutions become parasitically dependent on that endurance while
      reporting success.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      The qualitative phenomenology behind these failure modes is developed
      across <a href="https://thecrumple.zone">The Crumple Zone</a>; the formal
      research program on burden accounting and performance decomposition is
      hosted at <a href="https://kanav.net/research/">kanav.net/research/</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">Foundations</p>
    <h2>Start with these resources</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/use-cases">Which systems this is for</a>, by what the system
        decides, with the standards and first check for each.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/casebook">Casebook</a> for five public failures scored on six safeguards.
      </li>
      <li><a href="/glossary">Glossary</a> for terms and definitions.</li>
      <li>
        <a href="/mechanisms">Mechanisms catalog</a> for decision logs, escalation
        patterns, and reversibility tools.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/diagnostics">Diagnostics</a> for interactive tools you run on your
        own workflow.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/standards">Standards</a> for versioned requirements you can cite
        in policy, contracts, and audits.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/evals">Eval suites</a> for governance benchmarks and their test
        cases.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/field-notes">Field notes</a> for dated notes on outside changes
        that bear on the standards.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/start">Start here</a> for a path chosen by your role.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">Institute and Studio</p>
    <h2>How the Institute and Studio fit together</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Ethotechnics Institute</strong> is the public, open-access commons:
      standards, glossary, diagnostics, and field notes are free to use, cite, and
      extend.
    </p>
    <p>
      {studioSummary} The Institute publishes the open framework; the Studio applies
      it to specific systems, for {studio.clients}.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a
        href="https://ethotechnics.com"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        target="_blank"
      >
        Visit Ethotechnics Studio →
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">Citing this work</p>
    <h2>How to cite Ethotechnics</h2>
    <p>
      Ethotechnics Institute materials are published under
      <a
        href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        target="_blank"
      >
        CC BY-SA 4.0
      </a>. To cite the framework:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      Jain, K. (2026). <em
        >Ethotechnics: An open framework for the operational accountability of
        high-stakes automated systems.</em
      >
      Ethotechnics Institute. https://ethotechnics.org
    </blockquote>
    <p class="small muted">
      Individual glossary entries and patterns include their own citation
      metadata.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

